{"edges": [{"fromNode": "b1", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e1", "toNode": "b2", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b2", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e2", "toNode": "b3", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b3", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e3", "toNode": "b4", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b4", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e4", "toNode": "b5", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b5", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e5", "toNode": "b6", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b6", "fromSide": "bottom", "id": "e6", "toNode": "b7", "toSide": "top"}, {"fromNode": "b7", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e7", "toNode": "b8", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b8", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e8", "toNode": "b9", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b9", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e9", "toNode": "b10", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b10", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e10", "toNode": "b11", "toSide": "left"}, {"fromNode": "b11", "fromSide": "right", "id": "e11", "toNode": "b12", "toSide": "left"}, {"id": "e12", "fromNode": "b12", "fromSide": "right", "toNode": "b13", "toSide": "left"}, {"id": "e13", "fromNode": "b13", "fromSide": "bottom", "toNode": "pat", "toSide": "top"}], "nodes": [{"color": "5", "height": 70, "id": "title", "text": "Module 16 — Incident Response and Forensic Investigation\nConcept → first responder → do's and don'ts → handling process → AI/ML → SOAR → EDR → XDR → forensics", "type": "text", "width": 2400, "x": -1200, "y": -3600}, {"height": 700, "id": "b1", "text": "LO01 · THE CONCEPT  (pp4-9)\n\nIR = \"the process of taking ORGANIZED AND\nCAREFUL STEPS when reacting to a security\nincident\" — begins with first IDENTIFYING\nAND REPORTING; minimal damage, recovery\ntime, costs\n\n7 GOALS\n  actual vs false positive · Business\n  Continuity · reduce impact · analyze cause\n  · prevent future attacks · improve security\n  and IR · PROSECUTE illegal activity\n\nIRT = the group that collectively RESPOND,\nREMEDIATE, MITIGATE, RECOVER, COMMUNICATE\n  standing team costly → current employees +\n  a few dedicated members\n\n12 ROLES (pp6-8)\n  Management = FIRST to learn of an incident\n  InfoSec team: nature, category, scope\n  IT staff: traffic/logs/patches; FIRST\n    RESPONSE step\n  Physical security · Attorney (admissible\n    evidence) · HR rep (internal employee)\n  PR (Board, donors, vendors) · Financial\n    Auditor (assesses loss) · IR Officer\n    (oversees ALL) · IR Manager · Assessment\n    Team (prioritize by LOSS) · Custodians\n\nIR PLAN (p9) determines the future course;\nIRP = guidelines built BEFORE incidents", "type": "text", "width": 560, "x": -2400, "y": -3420}, {"height": 700, "id": "b2", "text": "LO02 · FIRST RESPONDER  (pp10-13)\n\n\"arrives FIRST at the crime scene and brings\nthe incident to the attention of others\"\n  end user · net admin · law enforcement ·\n  anyone in day-to-day network operations\n  value: early detection, source, impact,\n  collection + preservation\n  IRT works \"ON THE PRETEXT\" of the first\n  responder (as printed)\n\nDUTIES: reporting · alerting teams ·\n  containing · identifying the scene ·\n  collecting complete information ·\n  \"protecting, integrating, and preserving\"\n  evidence (as printed)\n\nTIME GAP (p12)\n  gap between occurrence and TRANSFERENCE\n  of evidence; gather WITHOUT MODIFYING\n  RUNNING SERVICES; uphold availability,\n  integrity, reliability\n\nFIRST RESPONSE RULE (p12)\n  \"UNDER NO CIRCUMSTANCES should anyone\n  EXCEPT FORENSIC ANALYSTS collect or\n  recover data\" — everything inside is\n  PROBABLE EVIDENCE\n\nBEFORE RESPONDING (p13)\n  review the IRP; before escalating document\n  IP + physical location · data type ·\n  activity timeline; containment differs\n  PER INCIDENT TYPE", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": -1740, "y": -3420}, {"height": 700, "id": "b3", "text": "LO03 · ASSESS + SEVERITY  (pp14-19)\n\nFUD = FEAR, UNCERTAINTY, DOUBT (p15)\n  do not panic · do not damage evidence\n  integrity · ESCALATE to management /\n  in-house forensics\n\nASSESSMENT (pp16-17)\n  actual vs false positive → category +\n  severity → immediate actions, minimize risk\n\n4 ALERT TYPES\n  False Positive — alarm, NO attack\n  True Positive — alarm, REAL attack →\n    act immediately\n  False Negative — NO alarm, real attack\n    (rules not defined properly)\n  True Negative — page prints \"AN ALARM IS\n    RAISED WHEN NO ATTACK IS DETECTED\"\n    = the FP definition under the wrong name\n\n6 CND CATEGORIES (p16)\n  Unauthorized Access · DOS · Malicious Code\n  · Improper Usage · Scans/Probes/Attempted\n  Access · Multiple Component\n\nLOW / MEDIUM / HIGH (p17)\n  low: password loss, failed scans, log-review\n    requests, garden viruses\n  high: DoS, break-in, Trojans/backdoors —\n    handle IMMEDIATELY\n\nDETERMINANTS (p19)\n  impact · CRITICALITY of the service ·\n  confidentiality … → urgency, expertise,\n  extent of response", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": -1080, "y": -3420}, {"color": "3", "height": 700, "id": "b4", "text": "LO03 · THE DON'TS  (pp20-27)\n\nCOMMUNICATE (p20)\n  who inside/outside, FAST; IRT/management;\n  contact must be an EXPERT\n\nCONTAIN (p21) — THE DILEMMA\n  DISCONNECT vs STAY = decided by the\n  FORENSIC EXAMINER or IR TEAM\n  disconnect → may LOSE evidence\n  stay → attack may SPREAD\n  do NOT notify all employees\n\nACCESS (p22)\n  LOCK AND KEY; secure nearby mobiles, CDs,\n  flash media — leaving one behind \"can\n  change the course of the investigation\"\n\nCOLLECT (p23)\n  who/what/when/how · IP · system time ·\n  name · running services; document changes\n  until forensics arrives\n\nRECORD (p24)\n  EVERYTHING, attacks AND false positives;\n  date/time + witnesses; CHRONOLOGICAL;\n  facts, NO speculation\n  weak: \"popups after the attack\"\n  ideal: \"Unknown popups on Chrome for\n  thirty minutes after the incident\"\n\nDON'T INVESTIGATE (p25)\n  non-expert collection → INADMISSIBLE;\n  worst case DIRECT LEGAL PUNISHMENT\n\nDON'T CHANGE STATE (p26)\n  ON stays ON · OFF stays OFF\n\nDISABLE AV (p27)\n  AV changes timestamps, auto-deletes\n  hacking tools", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": -420, "y": -3420}, {"height": 700, "id": "b5", "text": "LO04 · VISION → RECORDING  (pp28-36)\n\nGROUND RULES (p29)\n  restore normal state FASTEST · minimize\n  impact on others · no further incidents ·\n  ROOT CAUSE fast · assess damage · update\n  policies · COLLECT EVIDENCE\n\nNEED (pp29-30)\n  scenario · risk perception · business\n  advantage · LEGAL COMPLIANCE · policies ·\n  previous incidents\n  IR allows prevention but CANNOT PREVENT ALL\n\nVISION (p31)\n  purpose + scope; 7 plan-coverage items\n  (pass info · assess · contain/respond ·\n  restore · document · preserve evidence ·\n  report); 5 vision elements; PUBLISH in an\n  accessible repository AFTER APPROVALS\n\nPREPARATION (pp32-33)\n  IRT + training + tools BEFORE the event;\n  8 sysadmin duties (passwords, default\n  accounts, logging/auditing, patches,\n  backups, filesystem integrity)\n\nRECORDING (pp34-36)\n  tickets · SIEM/IDS/AV alerts; 8-step flow:\n  IT support → questionnaire → handler →\n  first responder lists compromised\n  elements → match old or CREATE record →\n  IRT takes over → manager classifies\n  HIGH FIRST, then medium, then low", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": 240, "y": -3420}, {"height": 700, "id": "b6", "text": "LO04 · TRIAGE → NOTIFY  (pp37-43)\n\nTRIAGE = 3 STEPS (p37)\n  analysis + validation · classification ·\n  prioritization\n  correlate indicators with LOGS + SYSTEM\n  FILES; classify by type\n  accurate indication ≠ incident (crashes can\n  be HUMAN ERRORS)\n\nCLASSIFICATION (p39)\n  nature · criticality · # systems · LEGAL\n  + REGULATORY; outside purview → other\n  departments; correct routing = faster\n  response + knowledge base\n\nPRIORITIZATION (pp39-40)\n  THE MOST CRITICAL DECISION;\n  NEVER FIRST-COME, FIRST-SERVED\n  IMPACT (# systems → idle staff → lost\n  productivity) vs URGENCY (usually the SLA)\n  each high/medium/low\n\nNOTIFICATION (pp41-43)\n  internal + external stakeholders;\n  DOCUMENTED MANAGEMENT APPROVAL first;\n  DO NOT HIDE info; external gets PARTIAL\n  picture AFTER approval\n  strategy weighs POLITICAL, TECHNICAL,\n  LEGAL, BUSINESS; reinvestigate details\n  BEFORE choosing strategy\n  duties: notify management of incident +\n  effects · inform likely-affected · seek\n  disclosure approval (denied → proceed\n  ANYWAY) · external support check", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": 900, "y": -3420}, {"height": 700, "id": "b7", "text": "LO04 · CONTAIN → POST-INCIDENT  (pp44-51)\n\nCONTAINMENT (pp44-46)\n  \"controlling the effect IMMEDIATELY AFTER\n  occurrence\"; evidence → FORENSICS now\n  shut down vs disconnect vs KEEP RUNNING\n  to monitor — depends on type\n  4 TECHNIQUES IN ORDER\n    1 disable services temporarily\n    2 remove from network (unknown vuln)\n    3 change passwords + disable account —\n      on ALL INTERACTING systems\n    4 COMPLETE BACKUPS of infected system\n  LOW PROFILE — do not tip off the intruder\n  (may erase everything)\n  standing guidelines beat stopgaps\n\nERADICATION (p47)\n  eliminate ROOT CAUSE (vulns, weaknesses,\n  misconfigurations); 9 countermeasures:\n  AV signatures · patches · audits · disable\n  services · passwords · kill access paths ·\n  clean rebuild · validate\n\nRECOVERY (pp48-49)\n  VERIFY BACKUP CLEAN BEFORE restoring;\n  course of action (resources, criticality,\n  cost-benefit) → monitor + validate\n  (pentests, watch for BACKDOORS)\n  rebuild with NEW OS; trusted backups\n\nPOST-INCIDENT (pp50-51)\n  limitations review · effectiveness check", "type": "text", "width": 620, "x": 1560, "y": -3420}, {"color": "4", "height": 700, "id": "b8", "text": "LO05 · AI/ML  (pp52-61)\n\n4 ROLES (pp53-54)\n  PROACTIVE DEFENSE — historical + TI data;\n    upgrades, patching, access steps\n  TRIAGE — severity/impact/relevance;\n    PRIORITIZE BY RISK AND URGENCY\n  AUTOMATED ANALYSIS — logs, system events,\n    network traffic; TI correlation\n  AUTONOMOUS RESPONSE — isolate devices,\n    BLOCK MALICIOUS IPs, patch, disable\n    accounts, remediate\n\nDETECTION (p55)\n  real-time volumes; PATTERNS + ANOMALIES;\n  models CONTINUOUSLY UPDATE\n\nTRIAGE (p57) — severity/criticality;\n  severe-first; fewer false alarms\n\nANALYSIS (p59) — TI correlation; PREDICT\n  incidents from history/trends\n\nRESPONSE (p60) — MILLIONS of events/day;\n  human delay is what adversaries exploit\n\n5 SOLUTIONS (p61)\n  SIEM (patterns, auto-alerts, workflows) ·\n  UEBA (insider, compromised accounts) ·\n  SOAR (AI/ML + workflow automation) ·\n  EDR (endpoint incidents) ·\n  XDR (ML/AI over collected data)\n\n  dashboards (Dynatrace, BigPanda) + the\n  p58 figure = NON-EVIDENCE; prose only", "type": "text", "width": 620, "x": -2400, "y": -2620}, {"height": 700, "id": "b9", "text": "LO06 · SOAR CORE  (pp62-69)\n\nSOAR = SECURITY ORCHESTRATION,\nAUTOMATION, AND RESPONSE\n  people + processes + technology;\n  cuts MTTD and MTTR\n\n3 CAPABILITIES (p63)\n  threat + vulnerability management\n    (formalized workflow)\n  security operations automation\n    (enrichment, prioritization)\n  security incident response (ONE CONSOLE,\n    no tool-switching)\n\n4 COMPONENTS (pp65-66)\n  Threat Intel — ingest/analyse; feeds by\n    IMPACT + SEVERITY\n  Orchestration — built-in/custom\n    integrations + APPLICATION PROGRAMMING\n    INTERFACES\n  Automation — log analysis, red-flag +\n    anomaly detection\n  Response — single-view dashboard\n\nINTEGRATION (p67)\n  SIEMs · firewalls · IDS · endpoint · TI\n  feeds · ticketing (+ scanners, UEBA, IPS,\n  EDR in the full prose list)\n\nAUTOMATION (p69) — 7 items:\n  strategize · CREATE PLAYBOOKS · orchestrate\n  · TI feeds · real-time comms · central\n  lifecycle view · post-incident review\n  (+ auto-triage in prose)", "type": "text", "width": 620, "x": -1740, "y": -2620}, {"height": 700, "id": "b10", "text": "LO06 · PLAYBOOKS + TOOLS  (pp71-86)\n\nPLAYBOOK (pp71-72)\n  \"PREDEFINED SEQUENCE of automated and\n  manual actions\" — 16 FIELDS IN ORDER:\n  Title → Description → Triggers → Context\n  + Gathering → Enrichment → Triage →\n  Automated Response → Manual Investigation\n  → Resolution → Communication →\n  Documentation → Escalation → Metrics →\n  Closure → Author/Reviewer → Version/Date\n  update CONTINUOUSLY\n\n6 EXAMPLES (pp73-79, prose only)\n  phishing: scan attachments/URLs; LABEL\n    VERIFIED, SLACK-MESSAGE the business\n  provisioning: OKTA or ACTIVE DIRECTORY;\n    deprovision ON EXIT\n  malware: isolate from active networks\n  alert enrichment: weed out false positives\n  threat hunting: standard protocols +\n    standard operating procedures\n  patching: AUTOMATE TICKET CREATION\n\nTOOLS (pp80-86, prose only)\n  Splunk SOAR — single source to observe /\n    understand / decide / act\n  Log360 — \"unified SIEM with DLP + CASB\";\n    MTTD/MTTR\n  ServiceNow (MITRE ATT&CK) · Heimdal ·\n  QRadar (chain-graph, privacy playbooks) ·\n  Swimlane · Demisto\n  all dashboards = NON-EVIDENCE", "type": "text", "width": 620, "x": -1080, "y": -2620}, {"height": 520, "id": "b11", "text": "LO07 · EDR  (pp87-108)\n\nendpoints: WORKSTATIONS, SERVERS, MOBILE\n  isolate · BLOCK malicious traffic ·\n  remediate BEFORE materializing\n  HOW IT WORKS (p89): detect → threat\n  actors (own list item) → contain →\n  investigate (endpoint OR network) →\n  remediation to PRE-INFESTATION state\n  11 FEATURES (pp91-92) incl. behavioral\n  baselines, TI/IOC integration, SIEM +\n  CSIR single interface\n  8-STEP INVESTIGATION (pp95-96):\n  collection → detection → alert generation\n  → prioritization (SEVERITY + IMPACT) →\n  investigation → hunting → containment +\n  eradication → remediation\n  HUNTING (p97): IOCs + anomalies; persist\n  UNTIL CONFIRMED HARMLESS\n  TOOLS: Cybereason · NetWitness (DWELL\n  TIME) · Falcon Insight · Malwarebytes ·\n  Cortex · HUNTRESS 24/7 · Symantec · Coro\n  · Bitdefender (GravityZone recorder)", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": -420, "y": -2620}, {"color": "1", "height": 520, "id": "b12", "text": "LO08 · XDR  (pp109-121)\n\nACROSS environments + layers; HOLISTIC;\n  EDR + network + cloud + email unified;\n  EDR-inside-XDR uses AI/ML per severity;\n  NDR telemetry; CROSS-DOMAIN hunting,\n  ONE CONSOLE\n  3 STAGES: INGEST (normalize) → DETECT\n  (AI/ML stealthy threats) → RESPOND\n  (severity priority, automated actions)\n  7 BENEFITS + 5 FEATURES (p111-112):\n  analytics cut alert volumes; HUMAN-\n  MACHINE teaming; signal-to-noise\n  TOOLS: Cynet auto XDR · Log360 · Vision\n  One · Falcon/Insight XDR · SENTINELONE\n  (DATA LAKE) · ExtraHop (NO VENDOR\n  LOCK-IN) · Cortex · Cybereason (MalOp) ·\n  Mandiant (dark web, OSINT) · Sophos ·\n  MICROSOFT XDR (agentless, self-healing)\n  TABLE 16.1 (p121):\n  EDR = TECHNOLOGY, endpoints, sig+behavior\n  XDR = TECHNOLOGY (EDR extension),\n  endpoints+cloud+networks, ML/AI\n  MDR = MANAGED SECURITY SERVICE,\n  analytics + HUMAN EXPERTISE, \"usually\n  MORE AUTOMATED than EDR/XDR\"", "type": "text", "width": 600, "x": 240, "y": -2620}, {"height": 700, "id": "b13", "text": "LO09 · FORENSICS  (pp122-129)\n\n\"METHODOLOGICAL PROCEDURES AND\nTECHNIQUES to identify, gather, preserve,\nextract, interpret, document, PRESENT\"\n  SIMULTANEOUS WITH CONTAINMENT;\n  IR contains, FORENSICS FINDS ROOT CAUSE\n  goal: incident + time + perpetrator +\n  mitigation steps\n\n5 OBJECTIVES (p123)\n  TRACK + PROSECUTE · forensically-sound\n  gathering · impact + INTENT · minimize\n  tangible/intangible losses · protect\n  against recurrence\n  before-and-after data; TIMELINE; budget\n  balances operations vs security\n\n3 USER GROUPS: Investigators · IT\nProfessionals · Incident handlers\n\n9 ROLES (pp124-125)\n  Attorney · PHOTOGRAPHER (must be\n  CERTIFIED for evidence photography) ·\n  Incident Responder · Decision Maker ·\n  Incident Analyzer · Examiner/Investigator\n  · Documenter · Evidence Manager (NAME,\n  TYPE, TIME, SOURCE per item) · Expert\n  Witness (authenticates, CROSS-EXAMINES)\n\n9 STEPS IN ORDER (pp126-127)\n  1 search WARRANT\n  2 evaluate + SECURE scene\n  3 COLLECT (neglect nothing)\n  4 SECURE evidence (loss weakens case)\n  5 ACQUIRE data (finds reason)\n  6 ANALYZE — THE MOST IMPORTANT PHASE\n     (log monitoring)\n  7 ASSESS evidence + case\n  8 FINAL REPORT (investigator's AND\n     suspect's actions)\n  9 TESTIFY\n\nPOST-ANALYSIS (p128)\n  found → PROSECUTION vs DISCIPLINARY;\n  not found → close or external agency;\n  SEVERE → law enforcement + FILE A CASE", "type": "text", "width": 620, "x": 900, "y": -2620}, {"color": "6", "height": 700, "id": "pat", "text": "THE MODULE'S FIVE TRAPS\n\n1 · TWO SEVERITY SCHEMES, NEVER LINKED\n  LO03 (pp17-19): LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH with\n  examples + determinants (impact,\n  criticality, confidentiality)\n  LO04 (p40): IMPACT (# systems) vs\n  URGENCY (usually the SLA), each\n  high/medium/low\n  → every \"what severity\" item tests WHICH\n  table is meant\n\n2 · TRUE NEGATIVE (p16)\n  prints \"AN ALARM IS RAISED WHEN NO\n  ATTACK IS DETECTED\" — the False Positive\n  definition under the wrong name.\n  Quoted verbatim, never repaired.\n\n3 · THE PAGE CONTRADICTS ITSELF\n  p6 CND Representative vs p7 HR Rep\n  p12 liability / integrating / pretext\n  p35 \"preempted questionnaire\"\n  p121 \"packages EDR and MDR\"\n  p129 repeats the TN label, drops the\n  contradictory definition\n\n4 · PRINTED DAMAGE, KEPT VERBATIM\n  mww.splunk · splunk.corn · wuw +\n  managengine · dynatrace.corn ·\n  solarwinds.corn · matwarebytes ·\n  paloanonetworks · trendrricro etc.\n  → prose Source: lines recorded as-is;\n  figure tiles omitted entirely\n\n5 · WHAT IS NOT PRINTED ANYWHERE\n  any dashboard number or graph value ·\n  the BigPanda p58 content · cloud-based\n  unified management (figure-only benefit) ·\n  Demisto feature list · the p71 truncated\n  triage row tail · any tool version", "type": "text", "width": 2040, "x": -2400, "y": -1820}]}